WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.078
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.938 --> 00:00:10.080
There are only two kinds
of people in this world.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.680
Those who consume illegal drugs and
those who knows someone who does.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.348
I'm not here to critic or
judge people who consume drugs.

00:00:20.348 --> 00:00:23.310
I have nothing against
the drugs themselves.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:27.680
My aim in giving this talk, however,
is to pull down the curtain, so

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.470
that you can see what is,
perhaps, not obvious.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.330
So that you all know
what is actually going on

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.260
before illegal drugs get into the US.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.905
I care deeply about this issue,
because I was born and

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.388
raised in the north of Mexico.

00:00:40.388 --> 00:00:44.290
My hometown is some 200
miles south of Texas.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:47.040
And it used to be a place in
which as a kid, I would bike and

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.690
skate around with my friends, and
people exercised in the street safely.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.500
But now,
everyone is living in constant fear.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.438
Children are not allowed to play outside.

00:00:57.438 --> 00:01:01.152
And literally everyone I know
could stand before you today and

00:01:01.152 --> 00:01:05.880
share a horrific story of how the drug
trade violence has damaged their lives.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.960
I have seen this other side.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.630
And I have kept quiet for way too long.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.548
I have lacked the courage to speak.

00:01:14.548 --> 00:01:16.490
And I have feared being the outlier.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.430
But I'm breaking my silence today
because people need to hear this,

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:21.764
especially here in the US.

00:01:21.764 --> 00:01:25.650
The US is the largest market for
illegal drugs.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.507
With only 5% of the world's population,

00:01:29.507 --> 00:01:33.590
it consumes over 30% of the global demand.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:38.074
And what's really interesting is that
the vast majority of the millions and

00:01:38.074 --> 00:01:42.008
millions of users are using the drugs for
recreational purposes.

00:01:42.008 --> 00:01:45.228
But only by buying,
they are creating a demand.

00:01:45.228 --> 00:01:49.292
And wherever this is demand,
there will be supply, and

00:01:49.292 --> 00:01:51.980
this supply is costing human lives.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:54.840
Let me illustrate this for you.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.939
Let's say you are a user and you feel like
getting high tonight or this weekend.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.710
So you would contact
your trusted dealer and

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:06.390
engage in a business
transaction with him or her.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.720
You see, it's a victimless crime.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.338
So, you get high, and no big deal.

00:02:13.338 --> 00:02:16.786
But what you don't see is that for
any dealer to be able to sell to you,

00:02:16.786 --> 00:02:19.169
they need to be working
with one of the cartels.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.340
And before cartels
supply to their dealers,

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.340
they either produced the drugs in
Mexico or imported them from Asia or

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:32.190
South America, and crossed them
to the US by land, air or sea.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:36.110
And at every step of
the chain there's bloodshed.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.100
Why is this the case?

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:43.190
Well, because this is the most
lucrative of all criminal activities.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.720
As a result,
it attracts the worst criminals to it.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.930
And criminals compete by force.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:54.180
So this is effectively a business in
which the most violent player wins.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.100
And what are they fighting over?

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:04.180
Well, territories, highways, ports, border
crossings, political influence, and so on.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.900
Because the real money to this
business is not in producing drugs but

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.130
in getting them from the source to
the end consumer here in the US.

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:13.800
And to do that successfully,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.070
cartels need to control their traffic
routes every step of the way.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.500
And to prevent smaller criminal groups
from growing and eventually competing

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:28.704
with them, they also set out to control
the smaller criminal activities like human

00:03:28.704 --> 00:03:34.500
trafficking, kidnapping, piracy,
extortion and even prostitution.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.060
To protect their huge profits,

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.850
they're killing not only the people
involved in the business, but

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:46.320
also journalists, law enforcers and
innocents, who refuse to cooperate.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.760
They even sometimes engage in
mass killings of civilians

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:52.890
just to create fear in the town.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:04:00.900
In the span of this hour of talks,
three people are killed just in Mexico.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:06.108
This is 60 lives every single day,
many of them are innocent men,

00:04:06.108 --> 00:04:10.020
women and children.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:17.068
The official death toll since
2007 stands at 160,000.

00:04:17.068 --> 00:04:22.198
This number is larger than that of
the Iraq and Afghanistan wars combined.

00:04:22.198 --> 00:04:26.580
This is the bloodiest conflict in North
American since the American Civil War.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:36.286
One morning back in the spring of 2010,
I found out that four very close

00:04:36.286 --> 00:04:41.328
member of my family had been
kidnapped by one of the cartels.

00:04:41.328 --> 00:04:46.070
Long hours of fear and
suffering turned into days.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.988
Days turned into weeks.

00:04:49.988 --> 00:04:52.588
We didn't know what was going on.

00:04:52.588 --> 00:04:57.168
And we still don't because
we never saw them again.

00:04:57.168 --> 00:05:00.480
The narcos disappeared them,
breaking my family apart.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.099
Now, by show of hands,

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:13.628
who here believes in making the world
a better place through our daily actions?

00:05:16.338 --> 00:05:18.290
I believe most people do, actually.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.490
In the US, every day it's more
common to hear about people becoming

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.630
vegetarian to save animals.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:28.458
People are buying cruelty free cosmetics,
third rate coffee, conflict free diamonds.

00:05:28.458 --> 00:05:31.133
And I deeply admire how
this heightened awareness,

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:33.240
how its supply chains is growing.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.400
But unfortunately, we haven't yet
picked up on the worst of them.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.298
One that is killing not animals,
but human lives.

00:05:39.298 --> 00:05:46.800
Because Americans are spending around
$150 billion on illegal drugs every year.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.367
An amount that is slightly higher than
what the US federal government spends on

00:05:51.367 --> 00:05:52.138
education.

00:05:52.138 --> 00:05:55.369
And five times larger than what the US
federal government spends on law

00:05:55.369 --> 00:05:56.090
enforcement.

00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:59.808
So, you can see why this problem is so
huge.

00:05:59.808 --> 00:06:03.605
And thanks to a very convenient
geographic location,

00:06:03.605 --> 00:06:07.410
Mexican cartels control
over 70% of the US market.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:14.076
The truth is that, with an incentive
of this magnitude, no government

00:06:14.076 --> 00:06:18.788
action in the US or Mexico will ever
be enough to mitigate the violence.

00:06:21.458 --> 00:06:24.751
Around three years ago,
Marie a hardworking,

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:29.773
lower-class woman who worked with me for
over six years, realized that

00:06:29.773 --> 00:06:36.120
one of the cartels was recruiting young
boys from her neighborhood by force.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.906
Being the mother of a 14 year old boy,
Marie panicked.

00:06:38.906 --> 00:06:41.790
So she took her whole family and
they walk away, they run away.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:47.130
For several months they lived at a very
remote and uncomfortable shelter.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:51.250
So, soon as they hear that their
neighborhood was relatively safe again,

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:52.160
the family return home.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:07:01.621
On the second night from their return, a
group of armed men entered their house and

00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:08.207
killed her boy in front of her, and
her whole family, just in retaliation.

00:07:13.428 --> 00:07:15.859
I completely understand that
some of you are thinking,

00:07:15.859 --> 00:07:19.240
how come the Mexican government
is not doing anything about this?

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:24.230
It is the Mexicans fault that they such a
corrupt government and weak institutions.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:24.770
And you know what?

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:25.930
That's fair.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:28.630
I accept our responsibility.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:32.310
And we are doing something about it,
but the pull is way too strong.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:36.130
How are we suppose to fight against those
hundreds of billions of dollars and

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:36.889
automatic guns?

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:41.038
The cartels just buy
their way around Mexico.

00:07:41.038 --> 00:07:43.480
And those who don't take
their money get killed.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:52.320
The fact we can't walk away from is
that every time we buy illegal drugs,

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:58.966
we approve, reward and
incentivize these criminal organizations.

00:08:03.332 --> 00:08:09.460
We approve, reward and
incentivize the narcos.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.400
So I stand before you today because
I desperately need your help.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:19.500
And because I know that each one of us
here could bring about a huge change.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:23.038
Think about this,
recreational users spend between 200 and

00:08:23.038 --> 00:08:27.020
$2,000 on illegal drugs every month.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:29.820
Just to give you a glimpse what
this money buys for the cartels.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:34.460
With $200 they can get 1,000
new rounds of ammunition.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:38.580
$500, a brand new AK47 rifle.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.570
$700 base, the monthly cost of
a full-time employee under that squad.

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:50.350
What if instead you or someone close
to you stopped buying illegal drugs?

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:58.390
You would singlehandedly help prevent
several kidnappings, extortions, or worse.

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:01.220
So if you're not the user,

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:04.960
I ask you to please have this conversation
with your friends, with your families.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:10.255
And if you are one,
please think twice before you buy again.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.882
Consider alcohol or legal wed instead.

00:09:14.882 --> 00:09:18.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Just don't lie to yourself

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.549
because now you know where you're
money will find its way through.

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:26.410
Eventually, if we succeed, my family,

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:31.020
people in my hometown and all over Latin
America would start feeling safe again.

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:37.234
Our children would go
back to playing outside.

00:09:37.234 --> 00:09:38.194
Thank you.

00:09:38.194 --> 00:09:45.641
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:45.641 --> 00:09:50.149
[MUSIC]

